From: bpereira <bpereira@cloudflare.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 00:48:21 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Speed up filling blurred rounded rects

The corner distance (x^e + y^e)^(1/e) cost three powf calls per vector,
and f32x8::powf carried a `// TODO: SIMD` and fell back to eight scalar
libm calls each. wasm has no hardware pow, so that was the dominant cost
in the whole filler.

Factor it instead: (x^e + y^e)^(1/e) == max * phi(min/max), where phi(t)
= (1+t^e)^(1/e) is bounded to [1, 2^(1/e)] on t in [0,1] and depends
only on the rect's exponent. A degree-7 fit covers it, and its
coefficients are degree-7 polynomials in the exponent, folded once per
span rather than per pixel. powf goes away entirely.

Also skip the p-norm away from the corners: x1 and y1 are non-negative
and outside the corner squares one is exactly zero, where the norm is
just the sum.

Accuracy: the phi fit is 4-11x worse than scalar powf in the worst case
(~0.35 alpha LSB vs ~0.03), but both are well inside half an LSB. The
old scalar path was only weakest at the tight-blur-large-radius corner;
elsewhere the fit and powf agree to ~0.001-0.006 LSB.

The fit is behind the poly-pnorm cargo feature, off by default. Without
it, the filler keeps the scalar powf per pixel, so the exact path stays
the default and an embedder opts in to the approximation.

The exponent fold lives in `phi_coefficients`, re-exported from `fine`
as #[doc(hidden)] so an embedder's accuracy test can sweep the fit
against exact pow. The table is generated and otherwise unreachable, so
without a seam nothing would catch a bad regeneration.

The alpha has a second derivative of order 1/std_dev^2, so for a wide
blur it is smooth at pixel scale. On axis-aligned transforms where a
pixel step is at most 1/16 of the standard deviation, paint_u8 now
evaluates one four-row column in every four and interpolates the three
between. That is a quarter of the evaluations, within one u8 step of
per-pixel evaluation from std_dev = 16 pixels up. Smaller blurs, rotated
or skewed transforms and the f32 pipeline keep the per-pixel path.

The subsampled painter visits complete 64-byte chunks and takes them as
array references, so the SIMD store needs no fallible slice conversion.
